---
name: review-delivery
description: A Crew handed back - check the hand-back against the brief, have it reviewed independently when the project wants that, send corrections, land it with `mate merge --review` or follow its pull request, close a scout, and close the task. Load it on a `wait-mate` or `pr-open` item, on `pr-merged:` or `pr-closed:`, and before you merge or stop any Crew.
---

# review-delivery

`wait-mate:` means the Crew stopped. It may be done, or it may be saying "I could not, here is why".
Never relay it to the captain as a result until you have checked it.
Start from the item `/usr/local/bin/mate wake shop` printed: the branch summary, the hand-back or report path, its `## For the next crew` section and the reviews recorded for it. Peek nothing.

## A ship

1. Rebuild the contract from `/ws/.mate/projects/shop/crews/<id>/brief.md` (`## Captain's words` with anything appended, then `## Build` and `## Acceptance`), never from the Crew's account of what it was asked.
2. Read `/ws/.mate/projects/shop/crews/<id>/handback.md` against it: every `## Acceptance` line has a row; each row says `pass` with a command and output that show it; `## Deviations from Build` holds nothing the captain's words did not ask for; `## Still open` holds nothing that was the task's to settle.
   A missing hand-back is a one-line send asking for it, not a reason to read the diff instead.
   A failed step 2 is settled before anyone reviews, whatever the review setting: a deviation from the captain's words, a part of `## Build` or of a spec it names that was dropped, or a choice `## Open decisions` or the captain owned, is a correction (see Corrections) or, when the Crew's choice would widen the contract, the captain's question. A reviewer never decides a deviation; it costs a review round to learn what the hand-back already says.
3. Whether an independent reviewer looks at it is the project's setting, which `wake` prints as `reviews:` and `/usr/local/bin/mate project review shop` prints in full. `off`: skip to Landing once step 2 holds. Undecided: ask the captain first, as rule 3 says, and record their answer before you go on. `on`: once step 2 holds, start an independent reviewer and end your turn: `/usr/local/bin/mate review shop <id> --id <review-id>`.
   With a tracker the review id is the issue and the round, `<issue>-r1`, then `-r2` for `--since` (`hvn-57-r1` reviews `hvn-57-trip-repo` and `hvn-57-f1` alike); without one, `<id>-r1`, shortening `<id>` to fit 24 characters.
   Without `--model` and `--effort` it picks the table's review profile by the size of the change; pass `--harness` alone to choose the reviewer's harness, and a full profile only when the change carries the blast radius `new-task` sizes as large.
4. When the reviewer hands back, read `/usr/local/bin/mate report shop <review-id> --summary`, then `/usr/local/bin/mate review shop <id> --check <review-id>`.
   A failed check is never approval: a stale review, an incomplete verdict or an unresolved finding needs a correction or a new review.

A summary, a diff stat or your own impression is not approval. Read the full patch yourself (`mate diff shop <id> --full`) only when the captain asks you to review directly.
Read-only git in the Crew's worktree is allowed for what `mate diff` does not print; anything that writes is not, and landing is `mate merge` alone.

## Corrections

A correction inside the contract is yours to send, in one line: a `fail` row, a criterion with no row, a deviation `## Build` did not allow, a diff that does not do what the captain's words ask.
One that would widen the contract goes to the captain first, as `wake` says, and the Crew waits.
A Crew that has handed back gets your line in a fresh session (`mate send` opens it) that starts from its brief, its hand-back, your line and the diff stat since the last review. Put what it must not repeat after the words `Already tried`.
When you tell a Crew to wait for something (a check, a build, another branch), tell it to wait once, with one blocking command and a long timeout. Never a sleep loop, never "check again every few minutes".

The fix after a review is reviewed from the commit that review saw, never from scratch:

- A fix that touches code, or only docs: `/usr/local/bin/mate review shop <id> --id <new-review-id> --since <review-id>`. The reviewer gets the diff since that commit and the earlier findings as a checklist, and reruns a gate only when the diff touches it.
- After a rebase or a merge of the default branch, compare the ranges yourself, read-only: `git range-diff <base>..<reviewed-sha> <base>..HEAD` in the worktree. When `--since` refuses because the branch was rewritten and the ranges show no semantic change, start a full review and tell the reviewer, in one `mate send` line with `--queue`, that you compared the ranges at `<reviewed-sha>` and it should confirm and rerun nothing.
- Two rounds of review at most. A third correction on the same theme is the captain's call: report the evidence and your recommendation, and the Crew waits.

## Landing

Once the review passes, land the work yourself:

```text
/usr/local/bin/mate merge shop <id> --review <review-id>
```

With the project's review `off`, land it once the hand-back checks out, with `/usr/local/bin/mate merge shop <id>` and no `--review`.
With `--review` it pins the commit the reviewer saw; either way it closes the Crew; report what it printed, in outcome words, and stop.
When memory `## Captain` says the captain merges this kind of work, report it as ready instead, naming the branch or the pull request.
A review that passed but left findings standing must have them recorded first: add a line under `## Queued` with `mate backlog add`, or name the tracker issue, and merge again with `--followups backlog:<text>` or `--followups <TRACKER-id>`.
On `merge refused: needs-rebase`, send the Crew one line, "rebase onto <default branch> and hand back with wait-mate", and merge after its next hand-back. Any other refusal: `mate-commands`.

A pull request ship (`--deliver pr`) hands back with `pr-open:`; review its branch exactly as above. It fixes corrections on the same branch and pushes; the same pull request follows. `mate merge` runs `gh pr merge` for it.
A watcher follows every open pull request and wakes you with `pr-merged:` or `pr-closed:`. When a Crew opened one without it and no console is open, start it: `/usr/local/bin/mate pr watch shop <id> <url>`.

- **`pr-merged:`** - run `/usr/local/bin/mate crew stop shop <id>`, which takes the merged pull request as landed, even after a squash or rebase merge. If the line says the checkout was `skipped:`, tell the captain their copy of the default branch is behind. Then take the next task.
- **`pr-closed:`** - it was closed unmerged. Find out why (its comments, through a scout if they are long), then either send the Crew the rework, telling it to reopen the pull request (`gh pr reopen`) and run `pr watch` again, or tell the captain it was turned down.

## A scout

Read `/usr/local/bin/mate report shop <id> --summary`, then the parts you need to verify its claims; the report is the whole deliverable.
A scout is done when its report answers every question under `## Deliverable`. Then:

1. Add to `/ws/.mate/projects/shop/PROJECT.md` what its `## Durable facts` says that the file does not, with the report as source, as `stow` says.
2. Relay the findings to the captain as findings, not "it's done".
3. Close it with `/usr/local/bin/mate crew stop shop <id>`. Keep it open only while you still need a follow-up answer from the same Crew.

## Closing a task

A Crew's task ends when you or the captain decide, never when the Crew says so; until `crew stop` or `mate merge` runs, it stays in flight.
A ship is closed when its work has landed. When the captain merged it, run `crew stop`; it finds the work landed.
Unlanded work is thrown away with `--discard` only on the captain's explicit word, and that records the Crew `failed`.
Then `/usr/local/bin/mate backlog done shop <id>`, and update the captain's tracker when the task lives there. When the task taught you something, `stow` says where it goes.
